   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_wt9snSTkmXqhMvXn",
   "name": "Vector Diagrams",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Vector diagrams"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How can I represent a vector visually?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- A **vector** has **both a size (magnitude) and a direction**\n\n  - You need to draw a **line** to show the **size of the vector**\n  - You also need to draw an **arrow** to show the **direction of the vector**"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2026/01/55921-representing-vectors-as-diagrams.png",
   "file": "assets/3a42f0aae6d2-55921-representing-vectors-as-diagrams.png",
   "alt": "Magnitude and direction of a vector",
   "caption": ""
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Vectors are written in** bold** when typed to **show** that they are a vector and not a scalar\n\n  - When writing a vector in an exam you should **underline** the letter to show it is a vector\n  - $a$ when **typed** and `bottom enclose a` when **handwritten**\n  \n    - You will **not lose marks** if you forget to underline vectors"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- If a vector **starts at *****A*** and **ends at***** B*** we can write it as $\\overset{\\rightarrow}{AB}$\n\n  - Here the arrow will **point toward *****B***\n  - Vector $\\overset{\\rightarrow}{BA}$ will have the **same length** but **point toward *****A***"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2026/01/29396-representing-vectors-as-diagrams-2.png",
   "file": "assets/6bfe5c603119-29396-representing-vectors-as-diagrams-2.png",
   "alt": "Vector between two points",
   "caption": ""
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What happens when a vector is multiplied by a scalar?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- When you **multiply** a vector by a** positive scalar**:\n\n  - The **direction** stays the **same**\n  - The **length** of the vector is **multiplied by the scalar**"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2026/01/27465-representing-vectors-as-diagrams-4.png",
   "file": "assets/b1fa3da6ccf2-27465-representing-vectors-as-diagrams-4.png",
   "alt": "Multiplying vectors by a scalar",
   "caption": ""
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- When you **multiply** a vector by a **negative scalar**:\n\n  - The **direction** is **reversed**\n  - The** length** of the vector is **multiplied** by the **number after the negative sign**"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2026/01/1645-representing-vectors-as-diagrams-5.png",
   "file": "assets/75c35fc560af-1645-representing-vectors-as-diagrams-5.png",
   "alt": "Multiplying a vector by a negative scalar",
   "caption": ""
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What happens vectors are added or subtracted?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- To **draw** the vector $a+b$\n\n  - Draw the vector $a$\n  - Draw the vector $b$ starting at the endpoint of $a$\n  - Draw a line that **starts at the start** of $a$ and **ends at the end** of $b$\n- To **draw** the vector $a-b$\n\n  - Draw the vector $a$\n  - Draw the vector $-b$  starting at the endpoint of $a$\n  - Draw a line that **starts at the start** of $a$  and **ends at the end** of $-b$"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2026/01/46039-representing-vectors-as-diagrams-6.png",
   "file": "assets/1d093b61e40a-46039-representing-vectors-as-diagrams-6.png",
   "alt": "Adding and subtracting vectors",
   "caption": ""
  }
 ]
}